  planner_response {
    response: "I\'ve fixed the issue preventing the app from starting! \n\nThe problem was twofold:\n1. When I updated the `package.json` earlier, the installation of the `express` dependency got overwritten, so it wasn\'t available when the server tried to boot.\n2. We were using Express 5, which uses a new version of the underlying URL matcher (`path-to-regexp` v8). In this new version, the syntax `app.all(\'/api/*\')` throws an error because wildcard splats now require parameter names. I updated this to use the more robust `app.use(\'/api\', ...)` instead.\n\nI\'ve correctly installed Express and restarted the development server. The application is now live and running on port 3000!"
    modified_response: "I\'ve fixed the issue preventing the app from starting! \n\nThe problem was twofold:\n1. When I updated the `package.json` earlier, the installation of the `express` dependency got overwritten, so it wasn\'t available when the server tried to boot.\n2. We were using Express 5, which uses a new version of the underlying URL matcher (`path-to-regexp` v8). In this new version, the syntax `app.all(\'/api/*\')` throws an error because wildcard splats now require parameter names. I updated this to use the more robust `app.use(\'/api\', ...)` instead.\n\nI\'ve correctly installed Express and restarted the development server. The application is now live and running on port 3000!"
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
